WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:11.383
When I was eight years old,
I had this ritual.

00:00:11.883 --> 00:00:15.470
After I finished my homework,
I'd head upstairs to my room,

00:00:15.512 --> 00:00:18.265
turn on my favorite Christina Aguilera CD,

00:00:18.265 --> 00:00:19.850
and sit on the floor

00:00:19.891 --> 00:00:23.812
surrounding myself with all this stuff
that was supposed to make me playful.

00:00:24.145 --> 00:00:26.690
I'd take out my Barbies one by one,

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:29.734
spread out my markers in every color,

00:00:29.776 --> 00:00:31.069
and then I'd wait.

00:00:32.487 --> 00:00:33.780
And I'd wait.

00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:38.827
And I'd wait until something
felt worthy enough,

00:00:38.869 --> 00:00:40.829
perfect enough to make.

00:00:41.413 --> 00:00:44.040
That moment barely ever came.

00:00:44.624 --> 00:00:46.084
Ever since I was a kid,

00:00:46.084 --> 00:00:49.045
play felt like something
that I had to earn.

00:00:49.045 --> 00:00:52.382
And when I finally did do it,
I took it seriously.

00:00:52.841 --> 00:00:56.761
My favorite game growing up
was emergency-room doctor.

00:00:56.803 --> 00:01:00.015
I’d force my younger brother
to scrub in, robes on,

00:01:00.015 --> 00:01:02.142
Beanie Babies fully prepped,

00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:04.019
and I was head surgeon, of course,

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:07.105
so there was no room
for mistakes in my OR.

00:01:07.772 --> 00:01:10.901
Most people don't become play skeptics

00:01:10.942 --> 00:01:12.819
until they're adults.

00:01:12.819 --> 00:01:16.573
But for me, even at eight,
I was questioning the point of it.

00:01:17.324 --> 00:01:21.286
So I ended up doing what
any reasonable skeptic would do,

00:01:21.328 --> 00:01:23.413
and I became a scientist.

00:01:23.788 --> 00:01:26.041
I got my master's in clinical psychology

00:01:26.082 --> 00:01:30.003
and spent almost the last decade
studying the science of creativity.

00:01:30.462 --> 00:01:35.383
And what I discovered surprised me
because creativity isn't just a talent.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.762
It is a core part
of all of our well-being,

00:01:38.762 --> 00:01:40.972
a pillar that I came to establish

00:01:40.972 --> 00:01:44.559
and become a fierce advocate for,
called “creative health.”

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.561
And one of the best strategies

00:01:46.603 --> 00:01:48.939
that we have to strengthen
our creative health

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.734
is exactly what I dismissed
as a kid: play.

00:01:53.193 --> 00:01:56.071
That's because when we play,
especially as adults,

00:01:56.112 --> 00:01:59.157
we unlock this unique type of flourishing.

00:01:59.491 --> 00:02:02.369
We have deeper fulfillment
and stronger resilience

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:04.955
and even more original ideas.

00:02:05.455 --> 00:02:07.499
But the type of play
that I'm talking about

00:02:07.499 --> 00:02:10.210
is probably not what we're all imagining.

00:02:10.251 --> 00:02:15.298
It's not just being silly or childlike,
or even starting up a brand new hobby.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.968
We’ve reduced play
to be this cherry on top

00:02:18.009 --> 00:02:19.719
of an already good life.

00:02:19.761 --> 00:02:23.848
But from a scientific perspective,
it is much more fundamental than that.

00:02:24.641 --> 00:02:28.561
Play is what happens anytime
we choose to do something

00:02:28.603 --> 00:02:31.147
without knowing exactly
where it's going to end up.

00:02:31.564 --> 00:02:33.566
There are no instructions
we need to follow

00:02:33.566 --> 00:02:35.443
or outcomes we need to achieve.

00:02:35.485 --> 00:02:37.028
Just two elements:

00:02:37.028 --> 00:02:39.864
intrinsic motivation and the freedom

00:02:39.864 --> 00:02:42.450
not to know the answer in advance.

00:02:42.951 --> 00:02:46.162
The problem is, we've come
to treat that type of play

00:02:46.204 --> 00:02:48.123
as worse than optional.

00:02:48.123 --> 00:02:50.834
It's something that we avoid
and even punish.

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:55.839
So much so that upwards of 70 percent
of adults around the world today

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.298
have stopped doing it.

00:02:57.924 --> 00:02:59.300
I first noticed this pattern

00:02:59.342 --> 00:03:01.469
as I was interviewing thousands of people

00:03:01.511 --> 00:03:03.763
about their current
relationship with play,

00:03:03.805 --> 00:03:06.766
and I kept hearing
the same thing over and over.

00:03:06.808 --> 00:03:09.769
They told me they didn't
have the time or the talent

00:03:09.769 --> 00:03:12.022
or the right tools to do it anymore.

00:03:12.063 --> 00:03:15.483
And when they finally did, it wasn't play.

00:03:15.525 --> 00:03:17.861
It looked a lot like my emergency room,

00:03:17.902 --> 00:03:21.948
something that was scheduled and optimized
with nothing left to chance.

00:03:22.741 --> 00:03:24.784
I know this might not seem
like a big deal,

00:03:24.784 --> 00:03:26.745
like, we're just having less fun.

00:03:26.745 --> 00:03:28.580
But it's deeper than that.

00:03:29.039 --> 00:03:32.667
We are living through
a “play-deprivation crisis.”

00:03:33.001 --> 00:03:37.088
We've systematically removed
everything that makes play what it is.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:39.883
The spontaneity, the freedom, the wonder,

00:03:39.924 --> 00:03:41.676
from all parts of adult life

00:03:41.718 --> 00:03:44.679
and replaced it with efficiency
and achievement.

00:03:45.055 --> 00:03:47.891
And in doing so, we're losing
the very survival skills

00:03:47.932 --> 00:03:49.642
that we need the most right now.

00:03:49.684 --> 00:03:54.355
Things like our capacity to adapt
and imagine and even feel alive.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:56.900
Play deprivation is tricky, though,

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.694
because at first we barely
notice it's happening.

00:03:59.694 --> 00:04:01.654
We just feel endlessly busy.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.823
But over time, without play,

00:04:03.865 --> 00:04:07.535
stress can compound
and then burnout can become chronic.

00:04:07.827 --> 00:04:10.205
And then we walk around
as playless adults,

00:04:10.246 --> 00:04:13.583
which ultimately creates
a playless society,

00:04:13.625 --> 00:04:16.461
one where our institutions can grow rigid

00:04:16.503 --> 00:04:18.630
and lonely and polarized.

00:04:19.089 --> 00:04:21.257
But we can change this, though.

00:04:21.299 --> 00:04:25.178
Not even just for ourselves,
but for future generations too.

00:04:25.178 --> 00:04:28.139
Because play isn't
something we age out of.

00:04:28.181 --> 00:04:30.016
It is a lifelong trait,

00:04:30.058 --> 00:04:33.144
which means no matter
how invisible it might feel,

00:04:33.144 --> 00:04:35.563
we can always restore it.

00:04:35.563 --> 00:04:38.399
And how we go about doing that
is much simpler

00:04:38.441 --> 00:04:40.235
and more expansive than you'd think.

00:04:41.236 --> 00:04:42.612
At my company Daydreamers,

00:04:42.612 --> 00:04:45.532
one of the things we got
extremely curious to figure out

00:04:45.573 --> 00:04:48.159
was finding the optimal way to play.

00:04:48.493 --> 00:04:50.662
Are you noticing a trend about me yet?

00:04:50.662 --> 00:04:54.332
We studied all kinds of people,
executives, postal workers,

00:04:54.374 --> 00:04:55.625
stay-at-home parents

00:04:55.625 --> 00:04:57.669
from 18 to 85.

00:04:57.669 --> 00:05:00.213
And we looked at how
they played not in a lab,

00:05:00.213 --> 00:05:02.382
but in the texture of their daily lives.

00:05:02.757 --> 00:05:06.469
And as we analyze the millions
of minutes of creative behaviors

00:05:06.511 --> 00:05:10.723
that we collected through pictures
and psychologically validated assessments,

00:05:10.765 --> 00:05:13.184
we found the opposite of my hypothesis.

00:05:13.643 --> 00:05:17.647
Play wasn't some list of actions
we could add to our calendar.

00:05:17.689 --> 00:05:19.399
It was an approach.

00:05:19.816 --> 00:05:23.862
The people who became creatively healthy
were doing something differently.

00:05:23.903 --> 00:05:27.282
They were playing all the time
in all these parts of life.

00:05:27.323 --> 00:05:30.118
Thinking playfully, expressing
themselves playfully,

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.579
even noticing when
it happened around them.

00:05:32.579 --> 00:05:37.000
And as they began to embody it,
their lives transformed.

00:05:37.041 --> 00:05:39.085
So while I wish I could stand here

00:05:39.085 --> 00:05:42.839
and give us one
play prescription to follow,

00:05:42.839 --> 00:05:45.216
that would be the antithesis
of what it is.

00:05:45.216 --> 00:05:46.426
What I can do, though,

00:05:46.467 --> 00:05:49.762
is paint a picture of what
a playful life could look like,

00:05:49.804 --> 00:05:52.056
because we don't need more stuff.

00:05:52.098 --> 00:05:56.269
We just need to start injecting it back
into how we already live.

00:05:56.561 --> 00:05:59.731
And the three places
to start are during work,

00:05:59.772 --> 00:06:02.942
before sleep, and in public.

00:06:03.693 --> 00:06:07.071
Let's start at work because most
of us get stuck in this linear,

00:06:07.113 --> 00:06:08.406
task-oriented thinking,

00:06:08.406 --> 00:06:11.409
churning through to do's
and in back-to-back meetings.

00:06:11.451 --> 00:06:14.120
But neuroscientists have found
that the spontaneous,

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.540
imaginative thinking
that's underneath creative play

00:06:17.582 --> 00:06:19.459
lives in a different part of our brain,

00:06:19.459 --> 00:06:21.336
called the default mode network.

00:06:21.336 --> 00:06:22.670
And that gets activated

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:25.924
when we do things we don't
normally consider productive.

00:06:26.299 --> 00:06:28.676
Playful people mind wander.

00:06:28.718 --> 00:06:30.136
They daydream.

00:06:30.178 --> 00:06:33.264
We often call that laziness,
but underneath the surface,

00:06:33.264 --> 00:06:35.934
our creative brain
is actually hard at work,

00:06:35.975 --> 00:06:39.520
connecting all these disparate ideas
that don't make sense yet.

00:06:39.562 --> 00:06:41.356
Even Einstein swore by this.

00:06:41.397 --> 00:06:45.944
He credited his most innovative ideas
not to doing math behind a desk,

00:06:45.944 --> 00:06:48.363
but to thought experiments.

00:06:48.404 --> 00:06:51.366
And right now, protecting
our nonlinear thinking

00:06:51.407 --> 00:06:53.409
matters more than ever.

00:06:53.409 --> 00:06:56.496
And that doesn't happen
through doing cringey icebreakers.

00:06:56.496 --> 00:07:00.250
So next time you see Mark staring off
into the distance during a meeting,

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:03.002
or even you want to look
away from your inbox,

00:07:03.002 --> 00:07:06.756
celebrate it because
your thinking is starting to play.

00:07:07.465 --> 00:07:09.342
OK, that sounds doable, right?

00:07:09.384 --> 00:07:12.303
But what happens
when we get home from work?

00:07:12.637 --> 00:07:16.140
Well, most of us feel exhausted,
so we reach for ease.

00:07:16.182 --> 00:07:18.810
We scroll [and] order food
at the click of a button.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.479
We want our lives to be frictionless.

00:07:21.479 --> 00:07:25.483
But our play and our lives need friction.

00:07:25.858 --> 00:07:28.903
There's actually a type
of positive stress called eustress

00:07:28.945 --> 00:07:32.865
that comes about when we do things
that require effort from us.

00:07:32.865 --> 00:07:34.575
That's why physically playing

00:07:34.575 --> 00:07:36.703
or making things with our bodies

00:07:36.744 --> 00:07:38.788
actually expands our energy

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:40.873
and even our perception of time.

00:07:41.416 --> 00:07:44.419
But we've come to engineer
the friction out of that too, though.

00:07:44.419 --> 00:07:45.586
We think playing at home

00:07:45.628 --> 00:07:48.339
is perfectly completing a page
of that adult coloring book

00:07:48.381 --> 00:07:50.883
or following a YouTube tutorial to a tee.

00:07:51.301 --> 00:07:56.306
Real play, though, is freer than that,
which can feel uncomfortable at first.

00:07:56.347 --> 00:08:00.310
It's experimenting with a bunch
of different ingredients without a recipe

00:08:00.351 --> 00:08:03.146
or doodling in the margins
of your notebook.

00:08:03.187 --> 00:08:04.689
It can be that simple.

00:08:04.731 --> 00:08:06.649
That's actually exactly what I was doing

00:08:06.691 --> 00:08:09.193
when I had my own creative breakthrough.

00:08:09.235 --> 00:08:12.113
Normally, I'd get home
from work and try to "relax,"

00:08:12.155 --> 00:08:14.782
or if I was feeling extra adventurous,

00:08:14.824 --> 00:08:18.202
I'd take out my notebook
and try to write something profound,

00:08:18.244 --> 00:08:20.288
neither of which are very playful.

00:08:20.747 --> 00:08:25.460
But one night while I was writing,
my pen moved to the left,

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:26.961
and then it made a circle.

00:08:27.295 --> 00:08:30.798
And then a bunch of random shapes
and patterns started to flow,

00:08:30.798 --> 00:08:35.845
and while I did feel uncomfortable,
I was also incredibly liberated.

00:08:36.429 --> 00:08:40.016
And when I came back to reality
and tried to make sense of it,

00:08:40.058 --> 00:08:44.145
I thought of my eight-year-old self,
sitting on the floor of her room,

00:08:44.187 --> 00:08:46.397
waiting for something worthy to make.

00:08:46.898 --> 00:08:50.777
And I realized in that moment
that she, like so many of us,

00:08:50.818 --> 00:08:51.986
got it backwards

00:08:51.986 --> 00:08:55.907
because we don't need to wait
for play to feel good to do it.

00:08:56.282 --> 00:09:01.412
Doing it in the face of resistance
and discomfort is how we begin.

00:09:02.372 --> 00:09:05.750
But we can't just play alone
at our desks or in our rooms.

00:09:05.792 --> 00:09:08.252
We need to play in public, too.

00:09:08.711 --> 00:09:13.007
The last and most insidious sign
of play deprivation is cultural.

00:09:13.007 --> 00:09:15.635
It's when we don't just
deprioritize it ourselves,

00:09:15.676 --> 00:09:18.179
but we actively punish it in the world,

00:09:18.221 --> 00:09:21.766
silencing music in our parks
or calling leisure time lazy.

00:09:22.183 --> 00:09:23.810
Almost a hundred years ago,

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:26.813
philosopher Johan Huizinga
warned us about this,

00:09:26.813 --> 00:09:29.649
suggesting that when a culture loses play,

00:09:29.649 --> 00:09:32.151
it can become brittle and polarized,

00:09:32.193 --> 00:09:33.945
unable to cope with change.

00:09:34.529 --> 00:09:37.657
I know that might seem familiar
and maybe even scary,

00:09:37.698 --> 00:09:41.077
but know that play
is inherently contagious.

00:09:41.411 --> 00:09:42.662
Research actually shows

00:09:42.703 --> 00:09:45.289
that just being in proximity
to people doing it

00:09:45.331 --> 00:09:47.625
can motivate us to do it, too.

00:09:47.667 --> 00:09:50.920
So we don't always need
to be the main character.

00:09:50.962 --> 00:09:53.714
We can just amplify it when we see it.

00:09:54.048 --> 00:09:56.092
I'm sure you've noticed
this happen before,

00:09:56.134 --> 00:09:59.720
when a crowd begins
to form around a musician,

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:02.682
but most of us end up racing past.

00:10:02.682 --> 00:10:03.891
So next time,

00:10:03.891 --> 00:10:06.853
I want you to take out your headphones
and listen for a moment.

00:10:06.894 --> 00:10:09.939
Maybe be the first
to laugh or clap or join in.

00:10:09.939 --> 00:10:12.650
Because when we make play visible,

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:15.987
it allows our communities
to come alive too.

00:10:16.696 --> 00:10:19.407
I know that even these
small moments of play

00:10:19.407 --> 00:10:21.534
can feel like an act of rebellion

00:10:21.576 --> 00:10:26.289
in a world that's convinced us
doing it as adults is unnecessary,

00:10:26.289 --> 00:10:27.999
and oftentimes they are.

00:10:28.624 --> 00:10:30.751
But it wasn't always this way.

00:10:31.377 --> 00:10:33.296
Since the beginning of our species,

00:10:33.337 --> 00:10:36.215
we have been making
these beautiful, weird,

00:10:36.257 --> 00:10:40.178
funny, interesting things
for no bigger purpose at all.

00:10:40.511 --> 00:10:44.932
Anthropologist Ellen Dissanayake
actually has a name for this capacity.

00:10:44.974 --> 00:10:47.518
She calls it “making special.”

00:10:47.935 --> 00:10:50.730
In her work studying
early human societies,

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:53.483
she also kept noticing
an interesting pattern.

00:10:53.983 --> 00:10:57.111
Every culture, without having spoken
to one another first,

00:10:57.153 --> 00:10:59.447
kept adding these playful touches

00:10:59.489 --> 00:11:01.908
to the most ordinary
aspects of their lives.

00:11:02.325 --> 00:11:04.994
They decorated their tools with intricacy,

00:11:04.994 --> 00:11:08.456
they made clothes and filled them
with beads and shells,

00:11:08.456 --> 00:11:11.959
they danced and told stories
and appreciated beauty

00:11:12.001 --> 00:11:15.630
for no functional or productive reason.

00:11:16.047 --> 00:11:17.548
Just because.

00:11:17.965 --> 00:11:20.092
So this exact capacity,

00:11:20.134 --> 00:11:22.470
to play, to “make special,”

00:11:22.512 --> 00:11:24.514
isn’t just inside of you --

00:11:24.805 --> 00:11:28.184
it’s wired into our entire species.

00:11:28.226 --> 00:11:30.978
It's always how we've stayed human,

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.855
how we've bonded and adapted

00:11:32.897 --> 00:11:36.192
and even made meaning
in life's most difficult moments.

00:11:36.609 --> 00:11:41.739
And I see that as our responsibility
to keep that part of ourselves alive.

00:11:42.198 --> 00:11:48.246
So we need to play again in any form,
in every part of life.

00:11:48.579 --> 00:11:51.958
Not necessarily because it will
lead us somewhere better,

00:11:51.958 --> 00:11:55.002
but because we are human

00:11:55.044 --> 00:11:56.963
and humans play.

00:11:57.463 --> 00:11:58.589
Thank you.

00:11:58.631 --> 00:12:01.092
(Applause)

